":1,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\"grade\":\"partial\",\"rationale\":\"The central requested content is correct. The answer says a new series added by Rule 485(a) post-effective amendment normally becomes effective 75 days after filing under Rule 485(a)(2), and notes that SEC acceleration is possible. It also correctly explains that this is a filing-effectiveness clock, not an end-to-end launch guarantee, because exchange listing, seed capital, operational readiness and other regulatory steps take their own time. It does not present 75 days as the rule for a new trust. However, the requested distinction for a new trust's initial registration contains a substantive legal error. It says initial registration 'generally becomes effective no earlier than 60 days after its initial filing under Investment Company Act § 8(a).' That is not an accurate statement of the regime: the 60-day period is the Rule 485(a)(1) period for other post-effective amendments, and ICA § 8(a) concerns registration of the investment company itself, not a 60-day Securities Act effectiveness clock for a new trust. Minor omissions: it does not mention that other material amendments use 60 days, that a designated later effective date is allowed (up to 95 days for a new series), or any filed example such as the Simplify filing. Because the core 75-day mechanism and the not-a-launch-guarantee point are right, but the new-trust distinction is materially wrong, the grade is partial.\"}","ttft_ms":3840,"type":"result","duration_ms":8051,"uuid":"bfb74892-10e8-4918-98b5-c109663a1e78","ttft_stream_ms":521,"time_to_request_ms":73,"first_content_frame_ms":521,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0,"credential_expires_at":"2026-11-28T06:36:39.484Z"}
